\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4286 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4247 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4207 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5319 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5359 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5398 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5714 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5753 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5793 \\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2033 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2327 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2643 \\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4286 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4247 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4207 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5319 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5359 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5398 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5714 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5753 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5793 \\,$ |" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083132 1071 "Calculate $\\textrm{LCM}(120, 90)$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083132 1071 "Calculate $\\textrm{LCM}(120, 90).$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083132 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $120$:![](images/fb/fb213f6ac09abbf31aacfdbfbd08b76dbea4199528abeb1c8041a73f0d7780ed.png)\nTherefore, $120 = 2^3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5$.\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $90$:![](images/03/034b3fe25edb2741abb035b8ca71a3c499fe6fae1a5975b4241d6379b819f19e.png)\nTherefore, $90 = 2 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5$.\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n120 &= 2^{3} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n90 &= 2 \\cdot 3^{2} \\cdot 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $120$ and $90$ is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(120, 90) = 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5 = 360\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083132 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $120\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n![](images/fb/fb213f6ac09abbf31aacfdbfbd08b76dbea4199528abeb1c8041a73f0d7780ed.png)\n\nTherefore, $120 = 2^3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $90\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/03/034b3fe25edb2741abb035b8ca71a3c499fe6fae1a5975b4241d6379b819f19e.png)\n\nTherefore, $90 = 2 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & ={2}^{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 90 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $120$ and $90$ is \n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(120, 90) = 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5  = 360.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083133 1071 "Find the lowest common denominator of $\\dfrac{4}{45}$ and $\\dfrac{7}{48}$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083133 1071 "Find the lowest common denominator of $\\dfrac{4}{45}$ and $\\dfrac{7}{48}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083133 1074 "The lowest common denominator of two fractions is the least common multiple of the denominators.\n\nSo, to find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $45$:![](images/5e/5e53857c25c4b7fd8dd1897dcd1b0608e48512d5a61a200a11a3f8a0ca92fcd2.png)\nTherefore, $45= 3^2 \\cdot 5$.\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $48{:}$![](images/e2/e21d5cf36f2f08fbbb46e1d923d84e0b02f10531c42c590ab7d3a813f34009dc.png)\nTherefore, $48 = 2^4 \\cdot 3$.\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n45 &= 3^{2} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n48 &= 2^{4} \\cdot 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $4$\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is\n\n$$\n2^4\\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5 = 720\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083133 1074 "The lowest common denominator of two fractions is the least common multiple of the denominators.\n\nSo, to find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $45\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/5e/5e53857c25c4b7fd8dd1897dcd1b0608e48512d5a61a200a11a3f8a0ca92fcd2.png)\n\nTherefore, $45= 3^2 \\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $48{:}$\n\n![](images/e2/e21d5cf36f2f08fbbb46e1d923d84e0b02f10531c42c590ab7d3a813f34009dc.png)\n\nTherefore, $48 = 2^4 \\cdot 3.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}45 & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\ 48 & ={2}^{4}\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $4$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is $2^4\\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5 = 720.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083134 1071 "Calculate $\\textrm{LCM}(30, 33, 20)$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083134 1071 "Calculate $\\textrm{LCM}(30, 33, 20).$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083134 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $30$:![](images/31/312d8c33355fb36260cce172ab20bf907637528d0a5d04dc4dd1eceb59d765dd.png)\nTherefore, $30 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5$.\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $33$. We easily deduce that $33 = 3 \\cdot 11$.\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $20{:}$![](images/7e/7e03a139fc3afa7df4d7f32ecf42f8f30d19bfe0415725f47fe5be63301899b5.png)\nTherefore, $20=2^2 \\cdot 5$.\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n33 &= 3 \\cdot 11 \\\\\n20 &= 2^{2} \\cdot 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $2$\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n- the highest power of $11$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $30$, $33$, and $20$ is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(30, 33, 20) = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 11 = 660\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083134 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $30\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/31/312d8c33355fb36260cce172ab20bf907637528d0a5d04dc4dd1eceb59d765dd.png)\n\nTherefore, $30 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $33.$ We easily deduce that $33 = 3 \\cdot 11.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $20{:}$\n\n![](images/7e/7e03a139fc3afa7df4d7f32ecf42f8f30d19bfe0415725f47fe5be63301899b5.png)\n\nTherefore, $20=2^2 \\cdot 5 .$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 33 & =3\\cdot 11 \\\\ 20 & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $11$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $30$, $33,$ and $20$ is \n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(30,  33, 20) = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 11  = 660.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493902 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493933 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493971 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493962 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493970 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493898 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493899 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493917 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493975 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083330 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493920 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493909 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493965 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493890 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493918 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493924 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493959 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083342 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493926 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083342 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493938 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083342 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493946 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083445 1050 480.0 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083466 1050 1380.0 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080063 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083690 1071 "$\\text{LCM}(98,175)=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083690 1071 "What is $LCM(98, 175)$?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083690 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $98:$ \n\n![](images/c7/c74979d4af6e34328dca1fab75fbf07c138c28ec8b93d5462d889f08ac6ea6c0.png)\n\nTherefore, $98=2\\cdot {7}^{2}.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $175:$\n\n![](images/56/56e885903dd09097a256b6740f7f6307ba2af3c0e43f32bb6456745acfb3e777.png)\n\n \nTherefore, $175=7\\cdot {5}^{2}.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}98 & =2\\cdot {7}^{2} \\\\ 175 & =7\\cdot {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $2$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $98$ and $175$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(98,175)=2\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}=2\\,450.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083973 1071 "In a factory, soda cans are filled with a mean volume of $12$ ounces of soda. However, $1%$ of cans contain no more than $11.5$ ounces of soda. Assuming that the volume of soda in each can is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the volume of soda in a can, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083973 1071 "In a factory, soda cans are filled with a mean volume of $12$ ounces of soda. However, $1%$ of cans contain no more than $11.5$ ounces of soda. Assuming that the volume of soda in each can is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the volume of soda in a can, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.900$ | $0.950$ | $0.975$ | $0.990$ | $0.995$ | $0.999$ | $0.9995$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $Φ^{-1}(p)$ | $1.2816$ | $1.6449$ | $1.9600$ | $2.3263$ | $2.5758$ | $3.0902$ | $3.2905$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083973 1074 "Let $X$ represent the volume (in ounces) of soda in a can. Then we have $X∼N(12,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 11.5)=0.01.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11.5) & =0.01 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11.5-12}{\\sigma }) & =0.01 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.01 \\\\ -\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.01).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)=2.3263,\n$$\n\nand therefore by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(1-0.99)={Φ}^{-1}(0.01)=-2.3263.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =-2.3263 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =4.6524 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{4.6524} \\\\  & ≈0.21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083976 1071 "A cellphone battery's average time from full to empty is $26$ hours. Additionally, $20%$ of the time, the battery lasts less than $20$ hours. Assuming that the duration of the battery is normally distributed, find the percentage of the times that a full charge lasts more than $30$ hours, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ |" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083976 1071 "A cellphone battery's average time from full to empty is $26$ hours. Additionally, $20%$ of the time, the battery lasts less than $20$ hours. Assuming that the duration of the battery is normally distributed, find the percentage of the times that a full charge lasts more than $30$ hours, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.05$ | $0.10$ | $0.15$ | $0.20$ | $0.25$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $Φ^{-1}(p)$ | $-2.3263$ | $-1.8808$ | $-1.6449$ | $-1.2816$ | $-1.0364$ | $-0.8416$ | $-0.6745$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p = Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $0.05$ | $0.06$ | $0.07$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.4$ | $0.6736$ | $0.6772$ | $0.6808$ |\n| $0.5$ | $0.7088$ | $0.7123$ | $0.7157$ |\n| $0.6$ | $0.7422$ | $0.7454$ | $0.7486$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083976 1074 "Let $X$ represent the time a full charge lasts. Then we have $X∼N(26,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>30).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<20)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<20) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-26}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-6}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{-6}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.14027 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.14027} \\\\  & ≈7.1291.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(30<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30) & =P(Z>\\frac{30-26}{7.1291}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.56) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.56) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.56) \\\\  & =1-0.7123 \\\\  & ≈28.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $28.8%$ of the times the battery lasts more than $30$ hours." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083977 1071 "The duration of feline pregnancies is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ days and a standard deviation of $5$ days. Additionally, $20%$ of the pregnancies have a duration lower than $59$ days. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to the nearest day.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186083977 1071 "The duration of feline pregnancies is normally distributed with a mean of $μ$ days and a standard deviation of $5$ days. Additionally, $20%$ of the pregnancies have a duration lower than $59$ days. Find the mean $μ$, rounded to the nearest day.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.05$ | $0.10$ | $0.15$ | $0.20$ | $0.25$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $Φ^{-1}(p)$ | $-2.3263$ | $-1.8808$ | $-1.6449$ | $-1.2816$ | $-1.0364$ | $-0.8416$ | $-0.6745$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186083977 1074 "Let $X$ represent the duration of a randomly selected pregnancy. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<59)=0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<59) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{59-\\mu }{5}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{59-\\mu }{5}) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{59-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)=-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{59-\\mu }{5} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 59-\\mu  & =-4.208 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈63\\,\\text{days.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186084484 1064 "The **lowest common multiple** (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of two integers is the smallest multiple that's common to both numbers.\n\nTo find the lowest common multiple of two small numbers, we can list all multiples of the two numbers and identify the smallest multiple that they have in common.\n\nFor example, let's find the lowest common multiple of $4$ and $6$. We start by listing all multiples of $4$ and $6$:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & → 04, 08, [math]12[/math], 16, 20, 24, … \\\\ 6 & → 06, [math]12[/math], 18, 24, 30, 36, …\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe smallest multiple in both lists is $12$ So $12$ is our answer, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)= 12\n$$\n\n**Note:** The lowest common multiple is also sometimes called the *least* common multiple." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186084484 1064 "The **lowest common multiple** (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of two integers is the smallest multiple that's common to both numbers.\n\nTo find the lowest common multiple of two small numbers, we can list all multiples of the two numbers and identify the smallest multiple that they have in common.\n\nFor example, let's find the lowest common multiple of $4$ and $6.$ We start by listing all multiples of $4$ and $6\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & \\,\\to \\,4,\\,8,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,16,\\,20,\\,24,\\,… \\\\ 6 & \\,\\to \\,6,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,18,\\,24,\\,30,\\,36,\\,…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe smallest multiple in both lists is ![](images/92/9285d37c7b455395acb42a54f78348bedfd8c9a72ad63d6e6ab7c2426148ea0d.svg) So $12$ is our answer, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)= 12.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The lowest common multiple is also sometimes called the *least* common multiple." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186084485 1064 "Now suppose that we want to find the lowest common multiple of two larger numbers. It would take a while to make long lists of multiples. Luckily, there is a faster method.\n\nLet's demonstrate by finding $\\textrm{LCM}(10,12)$. We start by writing the prime factorization of $10$ and $12$, writing any repeating factors as powers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 &= 2 \\cdot 5 = 2^{1} \\cdot 5^{1} \\\\\n12 &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 = 2^{2} \\cdot 3^{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write down each unique prime factor that appears in either of our decompositions. In our case, the unique prime factors are\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}, \\qquad {\\color{red}3}, \\qquad {\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nNow, for each number in our list, we look at *both* prime factorizations and we take the *largest* power shown for each prime factor:\n\n- The factors corresponding to ${\\color{red}2}$ are ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}$. Of these, the larger power is ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}$.\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}3}$. It is ${\\color{red}3}^{\\color{blue}1}$.\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}5}$. It is ${\\color{red}5}^{\\color{blue}1}$.\n\nFinally, we multiply the largest powers of each factor together, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLCM(10, 12) &= 2^{2} \\cdot 3^{1} \\cdot 5^{1} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186084485 1064 "Now suppose that we want to find the lowest common multiple of two larger numbers. It would take a while to make long lists of multiples. Luckily, there is a faster method. \n\nLet's demonstrate by finding $\\textrm{LCM}(10,12).$ We start by writing the prime factorization of $10$ and $12$, writing any repeating factors as powers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5={2}^{1}\\cdot {5}^{1} \\\\ 12 & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write down each unique prime factor that appears in either of our decompositions. In our case, the unique prime factors are \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}, \\qquad {\\color{red}3}, \\qquad {\\color{red}5}.\n$$\n\nNow, for each number in our list, we look at *both* prime factorizations and we take the *largest* power shown for each prime factor:\n\n- The factors corresponding to ${\\color{red}2}$ are ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}.$ Of these, the larger power is ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}.$\n\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}3}.$ It is ${\\color{red}3}^{\\color{blue}1}.$\n\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}5}.$ It is ${\\color{red}5}^{\\color{blue}1}.$\n\nFinally, we multiply the largest powers of each factor together, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(10,12) & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}\\cdot {5}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186085126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493530 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186085126 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493530 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186085127 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493718 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186085127 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493718 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186085968 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085973 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087437 1071 "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\n1. $-5x + y=3$\n2. $3x - y= -2$\n3. $3x= 5$\n4. $2x- 6y= 4$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186087437 1071 "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $-5x + y=3$\n\nII. $3x - y= -2$\n\nIII. $3x= 5$\n\nIV. $2x- 6y= 4$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087437 1074 "The *standard form* equation of a line is\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b$, and $c$ are all integers,\n- $a\\geq 0$, and\n- $a,b$, and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-5$).\n- Equation II is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n$\\underbrace{3x- y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}-2\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}$\nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$, $-1$, and $-2$) and don't have common factors greater than $1$. Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n$\\underbrace{3x}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}$\nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$ and $5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1$. Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n- Equation IV is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,-6$, and $4)$ have a common factor of $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186087437 1074 "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-5$).\n\n- Equation II is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3x- y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}-2\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3,$ $-1,$ and $-2$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3x}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$ and $5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n\n- Equation IV is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,-6,$ and $4)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087438 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form\n\n$$\nax+by = c\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$, and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and\n\n$$\na\\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 7x + \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\ny - 7x &= \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n- 7x + y &= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nHowever, it violates the rule\n\n$$\na \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x + y &= \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot (-7x + y) &= (-1) \\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\\n7x - y &= -\\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $c$ is a fraction, not an integer. To fix this, we multiply by $5$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x - y &= -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n5 \\cdot (7x - y) &= 5 \\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\\n35x - 5y &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b$, and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and\n\n$$\na\\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $35x-5y=-2$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186087438 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by = c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7x+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ y-7x 