rd form of the line is $x-y=-1.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087440 1071 "Calculate the equation of the straight line that passes through the points $(-1,3)$ and $(2,4)$. Write the answer in standard form." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186087440 1071 "Calculate the equation of the straight line that passes through the points $(-1,3)$ and $(2,4).$ Write the answer in standard form." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087440 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-3}{2-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(2,4).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3\\cdot (y-4) & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3y-12 & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-12 & =x-2 \\\\ 3y & =x+10 \\\\ -x+3y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+3y & =10 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x+3y) & =(-1)\\cdot 10 \\\\ x-3y & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-3y = -10.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087440 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\nm = \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} = (4 - 3)/(2 - (-1)) = \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(2,4)$. The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 4 &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 4 &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 2) \\\\\n3 \\cdot (y - 4) &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x - 2) \\\\\n3y - 12 &= x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3y - 12 &= x - 2 \\\\\n3y &= x + 10 \\\\\n- x + 3y &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + 3y &= 10 \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot (-x + 3y) &= (-1) \\cdot 10 \\\\\nx - 3y &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-3y = -10$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186087709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493927 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493955 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087710 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493934 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087710 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493940 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087711 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493922 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087711 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493928 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087712 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493891 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186087911 1050 720.0 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186088066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186088066 1071 "Express the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-3,-2)$ and $(-1, 4)$ in standard form. {{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186088066 1071 "Express the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-3,-2)$ and $(-1,4)$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186088066 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-(-3)}=\\frac{6}{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(-1,4).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, moving the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =3(x+1) \\\\ y-4 & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =3x+7 \\\\ -3x+y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+y & =7 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-3x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\ 3x-y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is \n\n$$\n3x-y=-7.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186088219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186088219 1071 "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\n1. $2x = 3$\n2. $-x + 2y = 3$\n3. $x - 2y = 0$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186088219 1071 "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $2x=3$\n\nII. $-x+2y=3$\n\nIII. $x-2y=0$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186088219 1074 "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{2x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2$ and $3$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($2$).\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-1$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{x-2y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{0}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $1,$ $-2,$ and $0$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186088991 1064 "The **standard form** equation of a line is\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b$, and $c$ are all integers,\n- $a \\geq 0$, and\n- $a,b$, and $c$ have no common factors.\n\nNote that we always write $x$ *before* $y$ on the left-hand side!\n\nLet's see some examples:\n\n- The equation $2x - y= -5$ is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n$\\underbrace{2x - y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{-5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}$\nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2$, $-1$, and $-5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1$. Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($2$).\n- The equation $-3x + y = 1$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-3$).\n- The equation $2x+4y = -10$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,4$, and $-10)$ have a common factor of $2$.\n- The equation $\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac13y = 6$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are not integers." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186088991 1064 "The **standard form** equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a \\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors.\n\nNote that we always write $x$ *before* $y$ on the left-hand side!\n\nLet's see some examples:\n\n- The equation $2x - y= -5$ is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2x - y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{-5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2,$ $-1,$ and $-5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($2$).\n\n- The equation $-3x + y = 1$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-3$).\n\n- The equation $2x+4y = -10$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,4,$ and $-10)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The equation $\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac13y = 6$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are not integers." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186088993 1064 "Let's now discuss how to convert the equation of a line to standard form.\n\nTo illustrate, we will take the line $y = 2x-1$ and convert it into standard form. We begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2x - 1 \\\\\ny - 2x &= -1 \\\\\n- 2x + y &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nHowever, it violates the rule\n\n$$\na \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + y &= -1 \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot (-2x + y) &= (-1) \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n2x - y &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b$, and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and\n\n$$\na \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $2x-y=1$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186088993 1064 "Let's now discuss how to convert the equation of a line to standard form.\n\nTo illustrate, we will take the line $y = 2x-1$ and convert it into standard form. We begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-1 \\\\ y-2x & =-1 \\\\ -2x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =-1 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a \\geq 0.$ So, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $2x-y=1.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186095580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095581 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186095580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095582 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186095580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095583 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186095580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095584 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186095580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095585 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186095580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493422 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186095580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493470 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186095580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493483 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186095580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493563 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186095580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493734 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186095580 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493470 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186096831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493919 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186096831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493961 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186097276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186097276 1071 "$\\int {cot}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186097276 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\cot^{2} (\\frac{π}{2} - x)dx$?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186097276 1074 "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) \\\\ {cot}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {cot}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\text{d}x & =\\int ({csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\text{d}x-\\int 1\\cdot \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)-x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186102686 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102688 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186104974 1078 #edb/ref 17592186104977 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186104986 1078 #edb/ref 17592186104988 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186105743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493896 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186105908 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493904 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186105908 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493949 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186106135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186106135 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $AB^(→)$, $AC^(→)$ and $AD^(→)$, given the points $A(1, 2,-1)$, $B(2, 3, 1)$, $C(4, 1, 2)$, and $D(1, 4, 2)$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186106135 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(1,2,-1)$, $B(2,3,1)$, $C(4,1,2)$, and $D(1,4,2).$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186106135 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+j+2k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =3i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(i+4j+2k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)-(3\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 3)+2(3\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-9-9+12 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-6|=6.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186106471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186106471 1071 "${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186106471 1071 "What is $(\\frac{3}{5})^{2}$?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186106471 1074 "To raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${3}^{2}=9$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{9}{25}.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186106473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186106473 1071 "${(-\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186106473 1071 "What is $(-\\frac{1}{7})^{2}$?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186106473 1074 "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{(-1)}{7}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-1)}{7})}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${7}^{2}=49,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{49}.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186109201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109202 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186109201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109203 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186109201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109204 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186109201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109205 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186109201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109206 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186109201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493446 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186109201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493719 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186109201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493757 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186109201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493765 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186109201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493790 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186109201 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493446 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110291 1071 "![](images/44/44aa56708e43f0518d7759e96b0770834f999998280c52910b2ca07cd607c1ab.png)\n\nGiven that $\\angle DOA$ is a straight angle, what is the value of $x$?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110291 1071 "![](images/44/44aa56708e43f0518d7759e96b0770834f999998280c52910b2ca07cd607c1ab.png)\n\nGiven that $\\angle DOA$ is a straight angle, what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110291 1074 "Note that $\\angle AOD$ is a straight angle and $\\angle BOC$ is a right angle. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOD={180}^{∘},\\,m∠BOC={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB+m∠BOC+m∠COD & =m∠AOD \\\\ x+{90}^{∘}+(2x-{30}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+{60}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={120}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={40}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110291 1074 "Note that $\\angle AOD$ is a straight angle and $\\angle BOC$ is a right angle. Hence,\n\n$$\nm∠AOD = 180^{∘}, m∠BOC = 90^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOB + m∠BOC + m∠COD &= m∠AOD \\\\\nx + 90^{∘} + (2x - 30^{∘}) &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x + 60^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 120^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110297 1071 "Calculate $\\left(\\dfrac{2x}{3}\\right)^4$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110297 1071 "Calculate $\\left(\\dfrac{2x}{3}\\right)^4.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110297 1074 "First, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2x}{3})^{4} = ((2x)^{4})/(3^{4})\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((2x)^{4})/(3^{4}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2^{4}x^{4}}{3^{4}} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{16x^{4}}{81}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110297 1074 "First, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2x}{3})}^{4}=\\frac{(2x)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2x)^{4}}{{3}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{4}{x}^{4}}{{3}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{16{x}^{4}}{81} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110298 1071 "Simplify $\\left(\\dfrac{y}{2}\\right)^3 \\left(\\dfrac{2y}{3}\\right)^2$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110298 1071 "Simplify $\\left(\\dfrac{y}{2}\\right)^3 \\left(\\dfrac{2y}{3}\\right)^2.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110298 1074 "First, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{y}{2})^{3}(\\frac{2y}{3})^{2} = \\frac{y^{3}}{2^{3}} \\cdot ((2y)^{2})/(3^{2})\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{3}}{2^{3}} \\cdot ((2y)^{2})/(3^{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{y^{3}}{8} \\cdot \\frac{2^{2}y^{2}}{9} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{y^{3}}{8} \\cdot \\frac{4y^{2}}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{3}}{8} \\cdot \\frac{4y^{2}}{9} &=  \\\\\n(4y^{(3 + 2)})/(72) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4y^{5}}{72} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{y^{5}}{18}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110298 1074 "First, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{2y}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{(2y)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{(2y)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{8}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2}{y}^{2}}{9} & = \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{8}\\cdot \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{8}\\cdot \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9} & = \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{(3+2)}}{72} & = \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{5}}{72} & = \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{5}}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110387 1071 "In the figure shown below, determine the measure of $\\angle EOA$.\n\n![](images/80/80f47745a289bf021a4566a58d1961c93bb216e2df900b8effae8f2dda3f1606.png)" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110387 1071 "In the figure shown below, determine the measure of $\\angle EOA.$\n\n![](images/80/80f47745a289bf021a4566a58d1961c93bb216e2df900b8effae8f2dda3f1606.png)" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110387 1074 "From the diagram, $\\angle COD$ is a right angle, so\n\n$$\nm\\angle COD = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\angle COD$ and $\\angle EOB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle EOB=m\\angle COD=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since we know that\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB=40^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm\\angle EOB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe can use the angle addition postulate to solve for $m\\angle EOA$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠EOA + m∠AOB &= m∠EOB \\\\\nm∠EOA + 40^{∘} &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠EOA &= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110387 1074 "From the diagram, $\\angle COD$ is a right angle, so\n\n$$\nm\\angle COD = 90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\angle COD$ and $\\angle EOB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle EOB=m\\angle COD=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since we know that $m\\angle AOB=40^{\\circ}$ and $m\\angle EOB = 90^\\circ,$ we can use the angle addition postulate to solve for $m\\angle EOA\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠EOA+m∠AOB & =m∠EOB \\\\ m∠EOA+{40}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠EOA & ={50}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110390 1071 "![](images/80/800f11f555f0c2be0e89aa26602cfe4a35a62f4fe2309dfe2a188f47138fb98b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $\\angle A$ is obtuse\n2. $\\angle B$ is a right angle\n3. $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are vertical angles" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110390 1071 "![](images/80/800f11f555f0c2be0e89aa26602cfe4a35a62f4fe2309dfe2a188f47138fb98b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\nI. $\\angle A$ is obtuse\n\nII. $\\angle B$ is a right angle\n\nIII. $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are vertical angles" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110390 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the diagram, $\\angle A$ and the angle with measure $130^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore,\n$m\\angle A = 130^\\circ > 90^\\circ$.\nConsequently, $\\angle A$ is an obtuse angle.\n- Statement II is false. From the diagram, $\\angle B$ and the angle with measure $50^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore,\n$m\\angle B = 50^\\circ \\neq 90^\\circ$.\nConsequently, $\\angle B$ is not a right angle.\n- Statement III is false. Angles $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are not vertical since they have a common side.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110390 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the diagram, $\\angle A$ and the angle with measure $130^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 130^\\circ >    90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\angle A$ is an obtuse angle.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the diagram, $\\angle B$ and the angle with measure $50^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm\\angle B = 50^\\circ \\neq    90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\angle B$ is not a right angle.\n\n- Statement III is false. Angles $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$  are not vertical since they have a common side.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110391 1074 "Since $\\angle S$ is a straight angle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle S=180^\\circ\n$$\n\nand since $\\angle T$ is a right angle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle T=90^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nSo, their sum is\n\n$$\nm\\angle S + m\\angle T=180^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ}=270^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sum of $\\angle S$ and $\\angle T$ is a reflex angle." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186110391 1074 "Since $\\angle S$ is a straight angle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle S=180^\\circ,\n$$\n\nand since $\\angle T$ is a right angle, we have \n\n$$\nm\\angle T=90^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nSo, their sum is \n\n$$\nm\\angle S + m\\angle T=180^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ}=270^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sum of $\\angle S$ and $\\angle T$ is a reflex angle." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493893 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493897 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110660 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493895 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110660 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493905 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110660 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493913 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110670 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493947 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110670 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493950 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493932 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493894 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493929 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493951 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493953 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110819 1050 420.0 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186110823 1050 600.0 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186111246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186111246 1071 "![](images/1f/1fab2b2158183c89fb48259408e3a1c684af657cd34d68d4b79e4773a4251f37.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, determine the measure of $∠GOH$ given that $m∠BOD={120}^{∘}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186111246 1071 "![](images/f7/f74bf7ac817f0e1612f09728f5198578e453dc7799d74402c7826d1416d86845.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, determine the measure of $∠GOH$ given that $m∠BOD = 120^{∘}$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186111246 1074 "From the diagram, $∠AOC$ and $∠EOG$ are vertical angles. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have\n\n$$\nm∠AOC=m∠EOG={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since $∠AOC$ is a right angle, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB+m∠BOC & =m∠AOC \\\\ {30}^{∘}+m∠BOC & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠BOC & ={60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, given that $m∠BOD={120}^{∘}$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC+m∠COD & =m∠BOD \\\\ {60}^{∘}+m∠COD & ={120}^{∘} \\\\ m∠COD & ={60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $∠COD$ and $∠GOH$ are vertical angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠GOH=m∠COD={60}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186111823 1064 "The **power of quotient rule** states that if we want to raise the quotient\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{y}\n$$\n\nto some power $n$, we must raise the numerator and denominator to the power $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^n=\\dfrac{x^n}{y^n}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{x^4}{y^4}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{x}{y})^{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x \\cdot x \\cdot x \\cdot x}{y \\cdot y \\cdot y \\cdot y} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{4}}{y^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186111823 1064 "The **power of quotient rule** states that if we want to raise the quotient $\\dfrac{x}{y}$ to some power $n,$ we must raise the numerator and denominator to the power $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^n=\\dfrac{x^n}{y^n}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{x^4}{y^4}.\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y} & = \\\\ \\frac{x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y\\cdot y\\cdot y} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186111840 1064 "When solving problems with angles, it is helpful to remember the following classifications:\n\n- a **null** angle has a measure of $0^\\circ$,\n- an **acute** angle has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ$,\n- a **right** angle has a measure of $90^\\circ$,\n- an **obtuse** angle has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ$,\n- a **straight** angle has a measure of $180^\\circ$,\n- a **reflex** angle has a measure between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$, and\n- a **full** angle has a measure of $360^\\circ$.\n\nIt is also helpful to remember that when two lines meet at a point, the opposite angles are called **vertical angles**. Vertical angles are congruent, and consequently they have the same measure." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186111840 1064 "When solving problems with angles, it is helpful to remember the following classifications:\n\n- a **null** angle has a measure of $0^\\circ,$\n\n- an **acute** angle has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ,$\n\n- a **right** angle has a measure of $90^\\circ,$\n\n- an **obtuse** angle has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ,$\n\n- a **straight** angle has a measure of $180^\\circ,$\n\n- a **reflex** angle has a measure between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ,$ and\n\n- a **full** angle has a measure of $360^\\circ.$\n\nIt is also helpful to remember that when two lines meet at a point, the opposite angles are called **vertical angles**. Vertical angles are congruent, and consequently they have the same measure." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186119959 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493892 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186119959 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493945 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186119961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493942 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186123524 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123528 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186124466 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493937 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186124488 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493915 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186125154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186125154 1071 "Consider the vector space $M_{2}(R)$ of all $2 \\cdot 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $C = {\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid a ∈ R}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $C$ is closed under matrix addition\n2. $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $C$ is a subspace of $M_{2}(R)$\n4. $C$ is **not** a subspace of $M_{2}(R)$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186125154 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $C$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $C$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $C$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186125154 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is an element of $C$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}ka & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $C$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186127057 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127058 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186127057 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127059 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186127057 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127060 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186127057 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127061 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186127057 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127062 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186127057 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493426 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186127057 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493516 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186127057 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493590 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186127057 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493652 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186127057 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493683 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186127057 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493516 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186129079 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493943 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186129312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493908 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186129596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186129596 1071 "Given the function $f(x, y, z) = xe^{y} - z$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x, y, z) = (1, 1, e)$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186129596 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{y}-z$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,e).$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186129596 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}-z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}-z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}-z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j-k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1,e) & ={e}^{1}\\text{ }i+(1){e}^{1}\\text{ }j-k \\\\  & =e\\,i+e\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨e,e,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨e,e,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186134262 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186134262 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186134262 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(21[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186134262 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(21[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n