ion space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1}{{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{2}b \\\\  & =-4b \\\\  & =-4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186134521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133164 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186134521 1074 "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{10\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 1+14\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\