b/ref 17592186163788 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186166848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493912 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186166848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493967 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186166877 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493903 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186167117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186167117 1071 "The students in a particular school are split among three classes, class $1$, class $2$, and class $3$.\n\n- Class $1$ contains $25%$ of the students in the school, and $55%$ of those students take biology.\n- Class $2$ contains $35%$ of the students in the school, and $40%$ of those students take biology.\n- Class $3$ contains $40%$ of the students in the school.\n\nIf $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, $80%$ of female students take biology, and $70%$ of biology students are female, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from class $3$ takes biology? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186167117 1071 "The students in a particular school are split among three classes, class $1,$ class $2,$ and class $3.$\n\n- Class $1$ contains $25%$ of the students in the school, and $55%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $2$ contains $35%$ of the students in the school, and $40%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $3$ contains $40%$ of the students in the school.\n\nIf $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, $80%$ of female students take biology, and $70%$ of biology students are female, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from class $3$ takes biology? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186167117 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $B$ be the event that a student takes biology, and ${C}_{1},{C}_{2},{C}_{3}$ be the event that a student is in class $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({C}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{C}_{1})=0.55 \\\\  & P({C}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(B|{C}_{2})=0.4 \\\\  & P({C}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})+P({C}_{2})+P({C}_{3})=0.25+0.35+0.4=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females take biology, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of biology students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|B)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student from class $3$ takes biology, which is represented as $P(B|{C}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.45)}{(0.55)(0.25)+(0.4)(0.35)+P(B|{C}_{3})(0.4)} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{0.36}{0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{C}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.7(0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})) & =0.36 \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.36}{0.7} \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35} \\\\ 0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775 \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.4}(\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775) \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & ≈0.592\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186167118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186167118 1071 "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1$, mode $2$, and mode $3$. They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $40%$ of their muffins, and $85%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $88%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $20%$ of all muffins was prepared with yeast A, and $87%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, what is the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is done with yeast A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186167118 1071 "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1,$ mode $2,$ and mode $3.$ They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $40%$ of their muffins, and $85%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $88%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $20%$ of all muffins was prepared with yeast A, and $87%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, what is the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is done with yeast A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186167118 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a muffin is made with yeast A, $G$ be the event that a muffin turns out well, and ${M}_{1},{M}_{2},{M}_{3}$ be the event that a muffin is cooked with mode $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({M}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(G|{M}_{1})=0.9 \\\\  & P({M}_{2})=0.4 &  & P(G|{M}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({M}_{3})=0.3 &  & P(G|{M}_{3})=0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})+P({M}_{2})+P({M}_{3})=0.3+0.4+0.3=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of muffins are made with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since $87%$ of muffins made with yeast A turn out well, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.87.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is made with yeast A, which is represented as $P(A|G).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(G|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(G|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.87)(0.2)}{(0.9)(0.3)+(0.85)(0.4)+(0.88)(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.174}{0.874} \\\\  & ≈0.199,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186169155 1078 #edb/ref 17592186169158 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186169161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169162 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186169161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169163 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186169161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169164 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186169161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169165 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186169161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169166 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186169161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493427 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186169161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493450 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186169161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493484 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186169161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493550 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186169161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493556 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186169161 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493427 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186171716 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) =   x-y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2 + z^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is c