 17592186045417 true] [17592186198950 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=y+x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n.\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{y-x}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,\\frac{1}{|y-x|}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}{e}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[v]_{1}^{10}\\,du \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =9[{e}^{u}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =9({e}^{4}-e).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186220231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220232 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186220231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220233 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186220231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220234 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186220231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220235 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186220231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220236 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186220231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493461 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186220231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493474 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186220231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493505 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186220231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493650 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186220231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493669 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186220231 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493474 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186221939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186221939 1071 "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-3)},\\,s>3\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\text{ }s>a.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186221939 1071 "For the function\n$G(s) = (1)/(s(s - 3)), s > 3$\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s)$, given that\n$L{e^{at}} = \\frac{1}{s - a}, s > a$." 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186221939 1074 "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-3)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwher