 onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186392340 1074 "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186394140 1071 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186394140 1071 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186394140 1074 "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=6{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6+5\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1}{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{41}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186394140 1074 "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=6{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6+5\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1}{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{41}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 false] [17592186400763 1071 "![](images/6d/6df3639cb222756221803446ebc735cf1e7cbcf4a