75 #edb/ref 17592186493606 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493735 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493889 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493889 1077 "selection" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493889 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493536 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493889 1079 #uuid "fb22af84-6e18-5551-a3f1-bbcb3eb8cfb6" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493809 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493890 1069 "q-103757" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493890 1071 "Find $\\text{LCM}(21,28,24).$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493890 1072 #uuid "02c3ac78-aa7f-5ad9-a0fe-7e15cb1f374c" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493890 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $21.$ We easily deduce that $21=3\\cdot 7.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $28:$\n\n![](images/50/502509ed3e4392c8c4e3e8fa0b0e579c096f0b04108091f4d18a6f0e5a879e2a.png)\n\nTherefore, $28={2}^{2}\\cdot 7.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $24:$\n\n![](images/cb/cbf192e5a1a1b1ac9db41bb9c8427ff8cdebeb697bcb618da87689a628b8f3af.png)\n\nTherefore, $24={2}^{3}\\cdot 3.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\ 28 & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 7 \\\\ 24 & ={2}^{3}\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $21$, $28$  and $24$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(21,28,24)={2}^{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot 7=168.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493852 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493891 1069 "q-109627" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493891 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-5,5)$ and $(-1,6)$, expressed in standard form?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493891 1072 #uuid "03ad1173-6dbd-512f-86a7-67df49248ff3" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493891 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{6-5}{-1-(-5)}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(-5,5).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+5) \\\\ 4\\cdot (y-5) & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(x+5) \\\\ 4y-20 & =x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-20 & =x+5 \\\\ 4y & =x+25 \\\\ -x+4y & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =25 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x+4y) & =(-1)\\cdot 25 \\\\ x-4y & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-4y=-25.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493837 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493892 1069 "q-186156" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493892 1071 "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493892 1072 #uuid "03b02208-a47d-5484-9a1d-0e9d1a5e06da" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493892 1074 "First, notice that the numbers $125$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{125}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493838 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493893 1069 "q-51810" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493893 1071 "${(\\frac{3wt}{8})}^{2}=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493893 1072 #uuid "0ada8c79-b2a0-5a2c-836a-ac533831ba5c" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493893 1074 "First, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3wt}{8})}^{2}=\\frac{(3wt)^{2}}{{8}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3wt)^{2}}{{8}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{2}{w}^{2}{t}^{2}}{{8}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{9{w}^{2}{t}^{2}}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493821 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493894 1069 "q-52736" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493894 1071 "![](images/ae/aec20235a2968f30f1ac3861d429a4102464a701fff0a90c13dae97334735273.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\nI. $∠A$ is null\n\nII. $∠B$ is acute\n\nIII. $∠C$ is obtuse" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493894 1072 #uuid "0bd5f670-a4a8-511a-9fe4-89d9e2250371" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493894 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the diagram, it follows that $m∠A={180}^{∘}>{0}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement II is true, and statement III is false. Indeed, from the graph we deduce  that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B+{100}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+m∠C & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of each of the angles $∠B$ and $∠C$ is less than ${80}^{∘},$ so these angles are acute angles.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493863 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493895 1069 "q-72635" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493895 1071 "${(\\frac{s}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{4}{t})}^{2}=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493895 1072 #uuid "12eb7643-9611-58a5-926c-4abbb115bd9f" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493895 1074 "We apply the power of quotient rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{s}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{4}{t})}^{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{{s}^{4}}{{2}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{2}}{{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{s}^{4}}{16}\\cdot \\frac{16}{{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{16{s}^{4}}{16{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{s}^{4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493887 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493896 1069 "q-68461" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493896 1071 "${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493896 1072 #uuid "151ea9a2-7fca-5107-a3ac-b15fd8b562e9" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493896 1074 "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using, ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${2}^{3}=8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493868 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493897 1069 "q-51811" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493897 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${(\\frac{wt}{2})}^{4}?$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493897 1072 #uuid "194005bc-f62b-59e9-a404-19d959ce797e" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493897 1074 "First, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{wt}{2})}^{4}=\\frac{(wt)^{4}}{{2}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(wt)^{4}}{{2}^{4}}=\\frac{{w}^{4}{t}^{4}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493846 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493898 1069 "q-325131" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493898 1071 "The weekly exercise time for a community is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ minutes and a standard deviation of $30$ minutes. Additionally, $60%$ of people exercise between $0.9\\mu $ minutes and $1.1\\mu $ minutes each week. Find the percentage of people who exercise less than $220$ minutes per week. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1020\\,$ | $\\,0.1003\\,$ | $\\,0.0985\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1210\\,$ | $\\,0.1190\\,$ | $\\,0.1170\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ | $\\,0.1401\\,$ | $\\,0.1379\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the people exercise less than $220$ minutes per week." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493898 1072 #uuid "1b9f0001-1223-5b0c-bd32-5cd32bc54dbc" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493898 1074 "Let $X$ represent the weekly exercise time of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{30}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<220).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =25.248 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{25.248}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈252.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<220)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<220) & =P(Z<\\frac{220-252.48}{30}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.08) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.08) \\\\  & =0.1401 \\\\  & ≈14.01%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $14.01%$ of the people exercise less than $220$ minutes per week." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493876 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493899 1069 "q-95073" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493899 1071 "A group of students took a test and got an average score of $81.$ Additionally, $5%$ of the students scored greater than $91.$ Assuming that the students' scores are normally distributed, find the percent of students with a score lower than $70,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0287\\,$ | $\\,0.0281\\,$ | $\\,0.0274\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493899 1072 #uuid "1e826c15-fd0c-50c0-b8b3-b16460ce62c6" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493899 1074 "Let $X$ represent the grade obtained by a student. Then we have $X∼N(81,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>91)=0.05.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =1-P(X>91) \\\\  & =1-0.05 \\\\  & =0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{91-81}{\\sigma }) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{10}{\\sigma }) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{10}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)≈1.6449.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.16449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.16449} \\\\  & ≈6.0794.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(X\\le 70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{70-81}{6.0794}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.81) \\\\  & =0.0351 \\\\  & ≈3.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.5%$ of the students scored lower than $70.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493889 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493900 1069 "q-78171" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493900 1071 "Given that  $({t}^{2}+t+2)({t}^{2}+2t+1)={t}^{4}+a{t}^{3}+b{t}^{2}+ct+2,$ calculate $a+b+c.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493900 1072 #uuid "222f2e72-f895-5b79-8f06-000077812dd5" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493900 1074 "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+t+2)({t}^{2}+2t+1) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}({t}^{2}+2t+1)+t({t}^{2}+2t+1)+2({t}^{2}+2t+1) & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+t+2{t}^{2}+4t+2 & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+3{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2}+5t+2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ $b=5,$ and $c=5.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+5+5=13.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493849 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493901 1069 "q-139533" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493901 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493901 1072 #uuid "236e6a34-eb12-52ba-848b-aa1ea35c9c6a" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493901 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493804 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493902 1069 "q-324300" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493902 1071 "Scores on a certain standardized exam are normally distributed with a mean of $75$ and a standard deviation of $8.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected student scores less than $68?$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ | $\\,0.2148\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected student scores less than $68$ is approximately\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493902 1072 #uuid "29065405-ccc0-5f24-b571-a168ed04c45b" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493902 1074 "Let $X$ represent the exam score. Then we have $X∼N(75,{8}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<68).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<68)=P(X\\le 68)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 68) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{68-75}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -0.88) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.88)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.88)=0.1894≈0.19.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student scores less than $68$ is approximately $0.19.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493862 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493903 1069 "q-149494" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493903 1071 "In a nature park, there are $4$ species of squirrels, each with the same population.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $1,$ $12%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $2,$ $5%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $3,$ $6%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $4,$ $3%$ are born in captivity.\n\nIf $35%$ of the squirrels are adult females, and $8%$ of the adult female squirrels are born in captivity, what is the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel born in captivity is an adult female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493903 1072 #uuid "2ad5265b-a872-53d0-b3f1-65585e111dd6" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493903 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a squirrel is an adult female, $C$ be the event that a squirrel is born in captivity, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},{S}_{4}$ be the event that a squirrel is of species $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ or $4,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- Each species has the same number of squirrels, so we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({S}_{1})=P({S}_{2})=P({S}_{3})=P({S}_{4})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.12 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.05 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{3})=0.06 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{4})=0.03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}⊔{S}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})+P({S}_{4})=0.25+0.25+0.25+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $35%$ of the squirrels are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of adult females are born in captivity, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.08.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel born in captivity is an adult female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.08)(0.35)}{(0.12)(0.25)+(0.05)(0.25)+(0.06)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.028}{0.065} \\\\  & ≈0.431,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493806 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493904 1069 "q-73733" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493904 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨-1,1,1⟩$, $b=⟨2,1,0⟩$, and $c=⟨k,-2,-3⟩$ are coplanar." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493904 1072 #uuid "2b5a6dbe-bec1-5af9-82e5-d252aa38eb1f" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493904 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ k & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ k & -3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 0)-(2\\cdot (-3)-k\\cdot 0)+(2\\cdot (-2)-k\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6-4-k \\\\  & =5-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 5-k & =0 \\\\ k & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=5.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493824 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493905 1069 "q-72638" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493905 1071 "${(\\frac{3}{4x})}^{2}{(\\frac{-2x}{5})}^{3}=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493905 1072 #uuid "2b75a1b6-f44a-5a5a-8342-d11fe3f5f88d" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493905 1074 "First, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4x})}^{2}{(\\frac{-2x}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{{3}^{2}}{(4x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(-2x)^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the power of product rule and multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{3}^{2}}{(4x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(-2x)^{3}}{{5}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{2}}{{4}^{2}{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(-2)^{3}{x}^{3}}{{5}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{16{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{-8{x}^{3}}{125} & = \\\\ \\frac{-8\\cdot 9{x}^{3}}{16\\cdot 125{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the quotient rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-8\\cdot 9{x}^{3}}{16\\cdot 125{x}^{2}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{9{x}^{3-2}}{2\\cdot 125} & = \\\\ -\\frac{9x}{250}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493829 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493906 1069 "q-99284" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493906 1071 "Find the value of $\\sqrt{59}\\cdot \\sqrt{59}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493906 1072 #uuid "2cb38511-839e-5fac-b7d9-ebdb7da5b4d2" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493906 1074 "First, we re-write the expression as a square:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{59}\\cdot \\sqrt{59}={(\\sqrt{59})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the square and the square root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{59})}^{2}=59.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493810 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493907 1069 "q-128150" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493907 1071 "$6-8.7=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493907 1072 #uuid "2d4d7236-1971-52db-b086-a77353f5fe00" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493907 1074 "Since $8.7$ is larger than $6,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem, which is to carry out the subtraction in the reverse order:\n\n$$\n8.7-6=2.7\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n6-8.7=-2.7\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493845 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493908 1069 "q-79732" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493908 1071 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493908 1072 #uuid "2dd7bee1-71b3-5685-9b9f-df2041d5d283" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493908 1074 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n5\\cdot 6+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-28.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+(-28)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-28 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493834 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493909 1069 "q-7317" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493909 1071 "Find the lowest common denominator of $\\frac{11}{30}$ and $\\frac{19}{64}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493909 1072 #uuid "2e11aa68-986c-58e0-bcdf-2852036febc7" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493909 1074 "To find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $30:$\n\n![](images/31/312d8c33355fb36260cce172ab20bf907637528d0a5d04dc4dd1eceb59d765dd.png)\n\nTherefore, $30=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $64:$\n\n![](images/44/447dca25b6cd3e19abd115869dac18c66b6066a37ef6181b4ac68e478aafb0b8.png)\n\nTherefore, $64={2}^{6}.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 64 & ={2}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $6$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is ${2}^{6}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5=960.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493805 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493910 1069 "q-154664" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493910 1071 "Which of the following equations has *no* solution?\n\nI. $|5z+1|=5$\n\nII. $|5z+1|-3=-1$\n\nIII. $|5z+1|=-6$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493910 1072 #uuid "2e7c0ce7-7ffd-571f-9543-de7ea37af680" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493910 1074 "An absolute value equation has no solution if, when the absolute value is isolated on the left side, the right-hand side is negative.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each equation in turn:\n\n- Equation I has a solution. The absolute value is already isolated on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side is non-negative.\n\n- Equation II has a solution. To see this, we first isolate the absolute value on the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5z+1|-3 & =-1 \\\\ |5z+1| & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side is non-negative, this equation has a solution.\n\n- Equation III *has no solution*. The absolute value is already isolated on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side is *negative*.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493813 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493911 1069 "q-139506" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493911 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $13$ and $8,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493911 1072 #uuid "39aef628-7059-5436-8f38-6e05c93c2909" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493911 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $13,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=13\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{13}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493885 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493912 1069 "q-148666" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493912 1071 "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two types of light bulbs: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $45%$ of the entire production, and $5%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $45%$ of the entire production.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $10%$ of the entire production, and $1%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $57%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, $3%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, and $41%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493912 1072 #uuid "39f657eb-aa97-5394-b0e6-ecba3bf80018" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493912 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.45 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.45 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.1 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.45+0.45+0.1=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $57%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.57.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $41%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D)=0.41\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective, which is represented as $P(D|{F}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\ P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{(0.03)(0.57)}{(0.05)(0.45)+P(D|{F}_{2})(0.45)+(0.01)(0.1)} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(D|{F}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.41(0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})) & =0.0171 \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.41} \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100} \\\\ 0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235 \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.45}(\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235) \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & ≈0.040\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493869 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493913 1069 "q-22532" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493913 1071 "${(-\\frac{a}{b})}^{3}{(\\frac{c}{d})}^{2}=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493913 1072 #uuid "3cbc76cf-73cd-53cf-89d7-15a5f1f46f2b" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493913 1074 "We apply the power of quotient rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{a}{b})}^{3}{(\\frac{c}{d})}^{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{(-a)^{3}}{{b}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{c}^{2}}{{d}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{-{a}^{3}}{{b}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{c}^{2}}{{d}^{2}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{{a}^{3}{c}^{2}}{{b}^{3}{d}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493857 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493914 1069 "q-162809" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493914 1071 "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4$ and $x+2y=-2$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩=\\lambda ⟨\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4 \\\\ x+2y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493914 1072 #uuid "3e1bedf1-a88d-51f4-8239-18dd7d6b26f1" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493914 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=x+2y=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493812 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493915 1069 "q-94638" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493915 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,3]\\to R$ and its subset $N$ containing all functions that return a nonzero value at exactly one point in $[0,3].$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $N$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $N$ is a subspace of $C[0,3]$\n\nIV. $N$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,3]$\n\n*Hint: Different functions from $N$ might give a nonzero value at different points in $[0,3].$*" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493915 1072 #uuid "3ec301c4-26d6-5f2a-9132-b21928f5ee67" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493915 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f({a}_{1})\\ne 0$ and $g({a}_{2})\\ne 0,$ where ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are distinct points in $[0,3],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({a}_{1})+g({a}_{1}) & \\ne 0 \\\\ f({a}_{2})+g({a}_{2}) & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)+g(x)\\ne 0$ at two distinct points. So, $N$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(a)\\ne 0$ for some $a\\in [0,3],$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\n$$\n\n \nfor any $x\\in [0,3].$ So, $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493861 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493916 1069 "q-68842" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493916 1071 "$2÷\\frac{2}{5}\\times 4=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493916 1072 #uuid "4227b37a-8e18-5afd-83bf-26143e32cd4d" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493916 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe divide and multiply, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2÷\\frac{2}{5}\\times 4 & = \\\\ (2÷\\frac{2}{5})\\times 4 & = \\\\ (2\\times \\frac{5}{2})\\times 4 & = \\\\ (\\frac{2}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{2})\\times 4 & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\times 5}{1\\times 2}\\times 4 & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times 5}{1\\times \\cancel{2}}\\times 4 & = \\\\ 5\\times 4 & = \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493823 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493917 1069 "q-133055" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493917 1071 "The output voltage of a particular model of laptop AC adapters is $19$ volts on average. However, $1%$ of adapters have a voltage greater than $20$ volts. Assuming that the output voltage is normally distributed, find the percent of AC adapters with a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493917 1072 #uuid "4286abc9-bbfc-5418-a3f8-c44338b9b405" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493917 1074 "Let $X$ represent the output voltage of a randomly selected AC adapter. Then we have $X∼N(19,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>19.3).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>20)=0.01.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =1-P(X>20) \\\\  & =1-0.01 \\\\  & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.99 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-19}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.99)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)≈2.3263.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =2.3263 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{2.32639} \\\\  & ≈0.42985.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>19.3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>19.3) & =1-P(X\\le 19.3) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{19.3-19}{0.42985}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le 0.70) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.70) \\\\  & =1-0.7580 \\\\  & ≈24.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $24.2%$ of AC adapters have a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493848 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493918 1069 "q-171159" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493918 1071 "$\\text{LCM}(9,15,2)=${{field-1}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493918 1072 #uuid "42ddf56f-a8d0-56db-815a-a130a857fd56" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493918 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- First, we find the prime factorization of $9.$ We easily deduce that $9={3}^{2}.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $15.$ We easily deduce that $15=3\\times 5.$\n\n- Then, we notice that $2$ is a prime number.\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 15 & =3\\times 5 \\\\ 2 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $9$, $15,$ and $2$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(9,15,2)=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5=90.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493886 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493919 1069 "q-352679" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493919 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493919 1072 #uuid "43534459-b512-57ce-a24a-a07661c93918" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493919 1074 "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So, we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3/2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{\\,3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493883 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493920 1069 "q-7552" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493920 1071 "Find $\\text{LCM}(7,19).$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493920 1072 #uuid "4852fb33-01b5-53c1-bda9-9f68b5cdfa3b" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493920 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers. Since the numbers $7$ and $19$ are prime numbers, they are already each their respective prime factorizations. \n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $19$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $7$ and $19$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(7,19)={7}^{1}\\cdot {19}^{1}=133.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493853 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493921 1069 "q-152221" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493921 1071 "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The sum of $4{x}^{2}+1$ and $2{x}^{2}$ is a binomial.\n\nII. The sum of $3{x}^{2}-2x$ and $5$ is a binomial.\n\nIII. The sum of a binomial and a monomial is **sometimes** a binomial." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493921 1072 #uuid "4a6f2377-d91b-5967-99d3-f6f62abbce5f" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493921 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n(4{x}^{2}+1)+(2{x}^{2})=6{x}^{2}+1,\n$$\n\nwhich is a binomial.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n(3{x}^{2}-2x)+(5)=3{x}^{2}-2x+5,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a binomial (it's a trinomial).\n\n- Statement III is true. The sum of a binomial and a monomial *could be* a binomial (see, for example, statement I). \n\n However, the sum of a binomial and a monomial is *not always* a binomial (see, for example, statement II). \n\n So, the sum of a binomial and a monomial is *sometimes* binomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493814 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493922 1069 "q-109615" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493922 1071 "Write $3x=y+1$ in standard form." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493922 1072 #uuid "4c641ffa-5f29-5085-9bd8-14bb6fe11280" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493922 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-y=1.\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $3x-y=1.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493874 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493923 1069 "q-162894" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493923 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+2y+3z$ on the intersection of the plane $x-y-z=0$ and the cylinder $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493923 1072 #uuid "4d8f93db-ca73-5128-94a3-1e0c56cdcf95" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493923 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+2y+3z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 2=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 3=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=∓\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})-z & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})-z & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $-\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its   maximum value is $\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493854 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493924 1069 "q-43578" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493924 1071 "Find $\\text{LCM}(14,18,63).$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493924 1072 #uuid "4dbbbcc8-62df-5e8e-bfa9-1313bcce5620" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493924 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $14.$ We easily deduce that $14=2\\cdot 7.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $18:$\n\n![](images/9e/9e7bddde8a287f6224b3e79f9fdacc3ac22d6db5a021b85df287e5e5fa321932.png)\n\nTherefore, $18=2\\cdot {3}^{2}.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $63:$\n\n![](images/93/936a74a3795c2a8ace97dfddd7d4f2f359acf8968ea4404c2711abb96d6a708d.png)\n\nTherefore, $63={3}^{2}\\cdot 7.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =2\\cdot 7 \\\\ 18 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 63 & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $14$, $18$  and $63$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(14,18,63)=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7=126.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493828 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493925 1069 "q-99298" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493925 1071 "Find the value of $\\sqrt[3]{63}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{63}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{63}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493925 1072 #uuid "4deafee5-88fa-505b-aa31-a2f75bd8228c" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493925 1074 "First, we re-write the product using the cube:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{63}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{63}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{63}={(\\sqrt[3]{63})}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt[3]{63})}^{3}=63.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493855 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493926 1069 "q-139246" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493926 1071 "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493926 1072 #uuid "53a86081-094e-5bab-a3ac-f318e3e80fd8" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493926 1074 "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is the following:\n\n![](images/00/0051f030962cd0a47503349f993ae1bb8faf90f47434213f9106fd30a69f6c60.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493842 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493927 1069 "q-216934" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493927 1071 "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $3y=5$\n\nII. $-x+7y=0$\n\nIII. $5x-3y=6$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493927 1072 #uuid "61b55285-bdfc-5092-a032-79dc8a8f2ea3" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493927 1074 "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{5}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$ and $5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($0$).\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-1$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{5x-3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{6}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $5,$ $-3,$ and $6$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493864 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493928 1069 "q-13339" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493928 1071 "Write $3y+3x+3=0$ in standard form." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493928 1072 #uuid "62452c7f-a9e2-5c51-81ef-baa2650df43c" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493928 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+3x+3 & =0 \\\\ 3x+3y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, $a,b,$ and $c$ have a common factor of $3.$ So, we divide by $3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3y & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{3x+3y}{3} & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x+y=-1.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493882 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493929 1069 "q-76777" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493929 1071 "![](images/64/64fcf64e043698366083e24b6041e2fb406b725671ab71c0c871c7e708517685.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\nI. $∠A$ is a null angle\n\nII. $∠C$ is a full angle\n\nIII. $∠D$ is a right angle" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493929 1072 #uuid "679aea70-862a-57d5-bcef-459256cb248c" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493929 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the diagram, $m∠A>{0}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the diagram, $m∠C<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. From the diagram, $m∠D={90}^{∘}.$ Therefore, $∠D$ is a right angle.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493836 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493930 1069 "q-139531" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493930 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $12$ and $7,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493930 1072 #uuid "6b27cf61-4032-5d19-a7e8-2cfee6fa397a" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493930 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=12\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $2\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493871 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493931 1069 "q-81148" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493931 1071 "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493931 1072 #uuid "6b62f170-a433-5de2-8209-ac0435d5fb1d" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493931 1074 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{\\text{i}}^{2}-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i}(4-2\\text{i}) \\\\ 1(4-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4-2\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4-2\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4-2\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4-2\\text{i}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493817 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493932 1069 "q-171812" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493932 1071 "![](images/be/be311b9b624fba745b93d7ac3cd67ace3bbe4ea0650b12fa900d3aac50b5bae2.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, the measure of $∠COF$ is{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493932 1072 #uuid "7352f5cb-3930-54b5-bef8-2eba5dc04e5f" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493932 1074 "The angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠EOD & =m∠EOB+m∠BOD \\\\  & ={82}^{∘}+{47}^{∘} \\\\  & ={129}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, $∠AOF$ and $∠EOD$ are vertical angles. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have \n\n$$\nm∠AOF=m∠EOD={129}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since $∠AOC$ is a right angle, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOF & =m∠AOC+m∠COF \\\\ {129}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+m∠COF \\\\ m∠COF & ={39}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493803 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493933 1069 "q-94978" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493933 1071 "The diastolic blood pressure of a group of people is normally distributed with a mean of $85\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and a standard deviation of $14\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected person from the group has a diastolic blood pressure greater than $77\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and less than $86\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}?$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2546\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2483\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2877\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2843\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2810\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3192\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3156\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4364\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5239\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5279\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5319\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5636\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5675\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5714\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493933 1072 #uuid "865ccf64-fc88-5f41-a8ec-576978864deb" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493933 1074 "Let $X$ represent the diastolic blood pressure of the selected person. Then we have $X∼N(85,{14}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(77<X<86).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(77<X<86) & =P(\\frac{77-85}{14}<Z<\\frac{86-85}{14}) \\\\  & =P(-0.57<Z<0.07)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.57<Z<0.07) & =P(Z<0.07)-P(Z\\le -0.57) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.07)-P(Z\\le -0.57) \\\\  & =Φ(0.07)-Φ(-0.57)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.07) & =0.5279 \\\\ Φ(-0.57) & =0.2843\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.57<Z<0.07) & =Φ(0.07)-Φ(-0.57) \\\\  & =0.5279-0.2843 \\\\  & =0.2436.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the randomly selected person from the group has a diastolic blood pressure greater than $77\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and less than $86\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ is approximately $0.24.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493811 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493860 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493934 1069 "q-214594" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493934 1071 "Express the equation of the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-3$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493934 1072 #uuid "8d0eef6f-b433-56d1-a89d-4aab29bca392" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493934 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{5}x+y=-3\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}x+y & =-3 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}x-y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply both sides of the equation by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}x-y & =3 \\\\ 5\\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}x-y) & =5\\cdot (3) \\\\ 2x-5y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\n2x-5y=15.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493844 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493935 1069 "q-186102" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493935 1071 "$-3.6-2.3=${{field-1}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493935 1072 #uuid "8f920e3a-0555-530e-885c-0505096145f6" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493935 1074 "The answer will be negative since $-3.6$ is negative, and we're subtracting $2.3$ from it.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem, which is to add their absolute values:\n\n$$\n3.6+2.3=5.9\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3.6-2.3=-5.9.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493815 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493936 1069 "q-162893" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493936 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=4x+y+2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=0$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493936 1072 #uuid "90c2cf43-9c0a-5736-b63a-0ceceb8b2660" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493936 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+y+2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 1=\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 2=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =0 \\\\ 2+(-1)+z & =0 \\\\ 1+z & =0 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =0 \\\\ -2+1+z & =0 \\\\ -1+z & =0 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2,-1,-1),\\,(-2,1,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2,-1,-1)$ | $(-2,1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $5$ | $-5$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1,1),$ and its minimum value is $-5.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493847 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493937 1069 "q-65263" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493937 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(-1,1,1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493937 1072 #uuid "970e7795-a408-5210-80aa-25f375509b0b" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493937 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{yz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\  & =\\frac{yz{e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{xz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xz{e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{xy}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy{e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1)(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(1)(-1)(-1)}}=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{e}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(-1,1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(-1,1,1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(1)(1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{-1}},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,1,0)=\\frac{1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{\\sqrt{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0}}}{2\\sqrt{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I  only'." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493822 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493938 1069 "q-139249" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493938 1071 "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493938 1072 #uuid "9a6a1966-1356-568d-b897-5f5f7b21ff10" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493938 1074 "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is the following:\n\n![](images/34/342a545437fc0884e69bb31d383a6e121c9cb0a1db08eab4d702cf9a0b296075.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493833 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493939 1069 "q-162816" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493939 1071 "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)=3x+2z+{y}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨3,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2⟩=\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493939 1072 #uuid "9aa1e55d-7de9-53b4-a8d5-5393a30ab5e5" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493939 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3x+2z+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,\\,2y,\\,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493879 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493940 1069 "q-109609" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493940 1071 "Write $y=-7x-4$ in standard form." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493940 1072 #uuid "9b7addf0-160c-54bc-8147-e2a24b57e5bd" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493940 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-7x-4 \\\\ 7x+y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $7x+y=-4.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493859 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493941 1069 "q-162814" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493941 1071 "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ and $2x-y+{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2x,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,-2z⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4 \\\\ 2x-y+{z}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493941 1072 #uuid "9d5803ec-f126-5445-94a3-4045259deba2" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493941 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=2x-y+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,-2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493840 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493942 1069 "q-71494" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493942 1071 "$(\\sqrt[3]{1,000})^{3}=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493942 1072 #uuid "9e1e6dba-6746-5b7e-9ed7-77e1ee7e8c76" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493942 1074 "The cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[3]{1,000})^{3}=1,000.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493818 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493943 1069 "q-102700" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493943 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z}$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,2,1).$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493943 1072 #uuid "a1c45efd-b454-595e-a933-aae318d15f95" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493943 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-{e}^{z}\\text{ }j-y{e}^{z}k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2,1) & =\\frac{2}{1}\\text{ }i-{e}^{1}\\text{ }j-(2){e}^{1}k \\\\  & =2\\,i-e\\,j-2ek \\\\  & =⟨2,-e,-2e⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-e,-2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493827 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493944 1069 "q-111223" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493944 1071 "Categorize the polynomial ${z}^{5}-3z$ based on its degree and the number of terms it has." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493944 1072 #uuid "a6b20681-3c0e-56d8-a0d2-12a0033bb72d" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493944 1074 "The polynomial ${z}^{5}-3z$ has two terms, so it is a binomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $5,$ so it is a quintic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quintic binomial." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493888 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493945 1069 "q-186155" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493945 1071 "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493945 1072 #uuid "aafcb24f-fde2-52b2-b44a-c7d7fddbc683" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493945 1074 "First, notice that the numbers $27$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{27}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493878 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493946 1069 "q-139242" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493946 1071 "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493946 1072 #uuid "ac482591-94cf-5da7-a1f4-becc288b5be6" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493946 1074 "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is the following:\n\n![](images/ff/ffbfb11f7cb0cef6cee8ccbb44aace9df2fdc0f831f5d78ad92a8f696b6ccffc.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493841 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493947 1069 "q-52647" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493947 1071 "![](images/a5/a5752cefaad576ffeb18408db80dac554a525a0e4137b46377fee225436da433.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $∠AOB≅∠COD$ and $∠BOC≅∠DOE.$ Given that $m∠AOB=2m∠BOC$ and that $∠AOE$ is a straight angle, $m∠BOC=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493947 1072 #uuid "b151647b-d6b1-5228-a002-474a2ce605db" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493947 1074 "Since $∠AOB≅∠COD$ and $∠DOE≅∠BOC,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB & =m∠COD \\\\ m∠BOC & =m∠DOE\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since $∠AOE$ is a straight angle, we have $m∠AOE={180}^{∘}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB+m∠BOC+m∠COD+m∠DOE & =m∠AOE \\\\ m∠AOB+m∠BOC+m∠AOB+m∠BOC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠BOC+m∠BOC+2m∠BOC+m∠BOC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 6m∠BOC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠BOC & ={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493851 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493948 1069 "q-71378" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493948 1071 "Find the value of $\\sqrt[3]{7}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493948 1072 #uuid "b22eb3b6-cc16-5099-ad8a-ae5922dfb11c" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493948 1074 "First, we re-write the product using the cube:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{7}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7}={(\\sqrt[3]{7})}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we use that  the cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt[3]{7})}^{3}=7.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493858 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493949 1069 "q-280770" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493949 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $x=⟨1,k,2⟩$, $y=⟨5,-2,3⟩$, and $z=⟨-4,k,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493949 1072 #uuid "b407ea24-4cac-5cb8-bbfb-b1a7b706dc73" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493949 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & k & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ k & 1\\end{aligned}|-k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot k)-k(5\\cdot 1-3\\cdot -4)+2(5k-(-2)(-4)) \\\\  & =-2-3k-k(5+12)+2(5k-8) \\\\  & =-2-3k-17k+10k-16 \\\\  & =-10k-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =0 \\\\ -10k-18 & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{9}{5}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493816 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493950 1069 "q-53173" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493950 1071 "![](images/d6/d607ba743afcc7d39299278e0d352e2c644b587480834a5c68cf2b46687b7f73.png)\n\nWhat is the value of $t$ given that $∠VOZ$ is a right angle?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493950 1072 #uuid "b6c23b91-4fda-5c9a-8297-f36bd4ddd1a4" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493950 1074 "Since $∠VOZ$ is a right angle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠VOZ={90}^{∘},\\,m∠VOW=m∠YOZ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can form an equation and solve for $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠VOW+m∠WOX+m∠XOY+m∠YOZ & =m∠VOZ \\\\ {30}^{∘}+t+t+{30}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 2t & ={30}^{∘} \\\\ t & ={15}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493880 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493951 1069 "q-52745" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493951 1071 "Let $∠S$ and $∠T$ be two adjacent angles. Given that $∠S$ is a straight angle and $∠T$ is obtuse, what type of angle is their sum?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493951 1072 #uuid "b814d590-16e2-55cf-89ba-ea5367397c7b" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493951 1074 "Since $∠S$ is a straight angle, we have \n\n$$\nm∠S={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n Since $∠T$ is obtuse, we have\n\n$$\n{90}^{∘}<m∠T<{180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of these two angles satisfies by the following inequalies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & <m∠S+m∠T<{180}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\ {270}^{∘} & <m∠S+m∠T<{360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sum of $∠S$ and $∠T$ is a reflex angle." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493856 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493952 1069 "q-153975" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493952 1071 "If $3|2x-5|+13=4,$ then $x=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493952 1072 #uuid "baeaa0a7-bfc7-5111-9e74-1fdaefb89ea5" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493952 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|2x-5|+13 & =4 \\\\ 3|2x-5| & =-9 \\\\ |2x-5| & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the right-hand side is negative. Therefore, the equation has no solutions." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493881 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493953 1069 "q-118297" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493953 1071 "Let $∠N$ and $∠S$ be two adjacent angles. Given that $∠N$ is a null angle and $∠S$ is straight, what type of angle is their sum?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493953 1072 #uuid "be894050-f250-5773-8720-f2e3adc2c8cc" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493953 1074 "Since $∠N$ is a null and a right angle, we have\n\n$$\nm∠N={0}^{∘}\n$$\n\nand since $∠S$ is a straight angle, we have\n\n$$\nm∠S={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, their sum is \n\n$$\nm∠N+m∠S={0}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sum of $∠N$ and $∠S$ is a straight angle." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493830 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493954 1069 "q-51576" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493954 1071 "Classify the polynomial $5{y}^{3}-2y+1$ according to its degree and the number of terms." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493954 1072 #uuid "becc645b-7ce0-5458-abaa-ac04d1cbd85e" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493954 1074 "The polynomial $5{y}^{3}-2y+1$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $3,$ so it is a cubic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a cubic trinomial." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493843 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493955 1069 "q-216943" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493955 1071 "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $4x+2y=14$\n\nII. $5x=10$\n\nIII. $5x-3y=1$\n\nWhich of these equations are written in standard form?" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493955 1072 #uuid "c460c44e-72fe-595b-9fd7-cf8fb6438c3d" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493955 1074 "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(4,2,$ and $14)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(5$ and $10)$ have a common factor of $5.$\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{5x-3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $5,$ $-3,$ and $1$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493808 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493956 1069 "q-160899" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493956 1071 "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{x}{4}\\le y\\le 2x,\\,1\\le xy\\le 4\\}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493956 1072 #uuid "c5c02db8-6f5e-5ac6-a24e-9e9016b1082e" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493956 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2y}{x})}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{-v/2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1/4}^{2}]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{4}]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\cdot {[-2{e}^{-v/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot ({e}^{-2}-{e}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493835 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493957 1069 "q-161046" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493957 1071 "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\text{__________},\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{z-1}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,10].$ What is the first component of $r?$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493957 1072 #uuid "c7e0b385-be5e-5415-b9aa-b210f7036677" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493957 1074 "![](images/04/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)=\\sqrt{z-1}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,10].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [1,10],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493873 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493958 1069 "q-175320" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493958 1071 "$(-4)\\times (2.3-0.1)=${{field-1}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493958 1072 #uuid "c9b4fb98-fcb9-51e9-9d1c-a0e33aad2c53" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493958 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\times (2.3-0.1) & = \\\\ (-4)\\times 2.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\times 2.2 & =-8.8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493832 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493959 1069 "q-103752" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493959 1071 "$\\text{LCM}(10,24,18)=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493959 1072 #uuid "ca4901aa-e3e7-56b2-8843-d65effed5ee5" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493959 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $10.$ We easily deduce that $10=2\\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $24:$\n\n![](images/cb/cbf192e5a1a1b1ac9db41bb9c8427ff8cdebeb697bcb618da87689a628b8f3af.png)\n\nTherefore, $24={2}^{3}\\cdot 3.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $18:$\n\n![](images/9e/9e7bddde8a287f6224b3e79f9fdacc3ac22d6db5a021b85df287e5e5fa321932.png)\n\nTherefore, $18=2\\cdot {3}^{2}.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\ 24 & ={2}^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\ 18 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $10$, $24$  and $18$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(10,24,18)={2}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5=360.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493826 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493960 1069 "q-175957" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493960 1071 "Expressed as an integer, $(-4)÷(\\frac{2}{5})\\times 6=${{field-1}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493960 1072 #uuid "d4dc1b86-0478-57c7-a48b-3ee42621256c" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493960 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)÷\\frac{2}{5}\\times 6 & = \\\\ ((-4)÷\\frac{2}{5})\\times 6 & = \\\\ (\\frac{-4}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{2})\\times 6 & = \\\\ (\\frac{(-4)\\times 5}{1\\times 2})\\times 6 & = \\\\ (-\\frac{20}{2})\\times 6 & = \\\\ (-10)\\times 6 & = \\\\ -60\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493872 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493961 1069 "q-352652" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493961 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493961 1072 #uuid "d59d88c8-9c62-51d9-bd12-48f5c362cc67" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493961 1074 "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493839 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493962 1069 "q-95126" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493962 1071 "The lengths of babies at birth in a given population are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{cm}$ and a standard deviation of $6\\,\\text{cm}.$ Additionally, the length of $60%$ of the newborns falls between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{cm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{cm}.$ Find the mean $\\mu $ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493962 1072 #uuid "d916a01a-c116-520b-be92-bf93eddd6997" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493962 1074 "Let $X$ represent the length of a randomly selected newborn. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{6}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.6.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.6 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.6 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.3 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.3 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.8 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6}) & =0.8 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6}) & =0.8 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{6} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.8)≈0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =5.0496 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{5.0496}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈50.50\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493877 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493963 1069 "q-78170" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493963 1071 "Given that  $({p}^{2}+p+1)({p}^{2}+3p+1)={p}^{4}+a{p}^{3}+b{p}^{2}+cp+1,$ calculate $a+b+c.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493963 1072 #uuid "d9aae8a4-653e-5752-9c3b-a882c899846f" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493963 1074 "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({p}^{2}+p+1)({p}^{2}+3p+1) & = \\\\ {p}^{2}({p}^{2}+3p+1)+p({p}^{2}+3p+1)+1({p}^{2}+3p+1) & = \\\\ {p}^{4}+3{p}^{3}+{p}^{2}+{p}^{3}+3{p}^{2}+p+{p}^{2}+3p+1 & = \\\\ {p}^{4}+4{p}^{3}+5{p}^{2}+4p+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ $b=5,$ and $c=4.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=4+5+4=13.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493825 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493964 1069 "q-162897" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493964 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+4y-2z$ on the intersection of the plane $2x-4y-2z=1$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493964 1072 #uuid "daa44e18-10db-5d53-b4fb-045b6b617679" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493964 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x+4y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-4y-2z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,4,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-4,-2⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 4=-4\\lambda  \\\\ -2=-2\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{2}{\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $z$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{8}{{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,z=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $z=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y-2z & =1 \\\\ 2(1)-4y-2(-1) & =1 \\\\ -4y & =-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $z=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y-2z & =1 \\\\ 2(-1)-4y-2(1) & =1 \\\\ -4y & =5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,\\frac{3}{4},-1),\\,(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,\\frac{3}{4},-1)$ | $(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $7$ | $-9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1),$ and its minimum value is $-9.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493819 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493965 1069 "q-7554" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493965 1071 "Find the lowest common denominator of $\\frac{2}{5}$ and $\\frac{8}{11}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493965 1072 #uuid "dc2e2da4-0526-5cbb-b99c-ff4e18d482b4" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493965 1074 "To find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators. \n\nSince the denominators $5$ and $11$ are prime numbers, they are already represented by their respective prime factorizations. \n\nNow, we write down each prime factor, and we raise each factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $11$ is also $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is ${5}^{1}\\cdot {11}^{1}=55.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493820 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493966 1069 "q-112321" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493966 1071 "If $7|2x-5|+1=15,$ then $x=$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493966 1072 #uuid "ddcdce51-4bc7-55a4-81e7-a74a7a6b3b12" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493966 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7|2x-5|+1 & =15 \\\\ 7|2x-5| & =14 \\\\ |2x-5| & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $|2x-5|=2x-5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-5| & =2 \\\\ 2x-5 & =2 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|2x-5|=-(2x-5),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-5| & =2 \\\\ -(2x-5) & =2 \\\\ -2x+5 & =2 \\\\ -2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493866 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493967 1069 "q-149493" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493967 1071 "A volleyball school for children splits the players into three teams, team $1$, team $2,$ and team $3.$ \n\n- $44%$ of children are in team $1,$ and $23%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $32%$ of children are in team $2,$ and $26%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $24%$ of children are in team $3.$\n\nIf $7%$ of all children can play in the blocker position, $40%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, and $10%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child in team $3$ has been playing for over two years? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493967 1072 #uuid "deb0627a-b1f4-5ecd-95e2-372ffa642f2c" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493967 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in the blocker position, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over two years, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.23 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.26 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.44+0.32+0.24=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $7%$ of children can play in the blocker position, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.07.\n$$\n\n- Since $40%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|I)=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child in team 3 has been playing for over two years, which is represented as $P(I|{T}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\ P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.07)}{(0.23)(0.44)+(0.26)(0.32)+P(I|{T}_{3})(0.24)} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(I|{T}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1(0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})) & =0.028 \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1} \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25} \\\\ 0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844 \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.24}(\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844) \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & ≈0.398\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493807 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493968 1069 "q-140566" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493968 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493968 1072 #uuid "dfb0578c-1343-53e2-a376-085feaa6ce26" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493968 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493831 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493969 1069 "q-95286" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493969 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $6\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $6$ distinct singular values.\n\nII. Any $8\\times 9$ matrix $A$ has at most $9$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ has at most two non-negative distinct singular values." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493969 1072 #uuid "dfba6eb1-d0b6-5cbc-ac16-d682a2e1cc93" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493969 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $6\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $8\\times 9$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $9\\times 8$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $9\\times 9$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{9\\times 8}\\times {A}_{8\\times 9}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{9\\times 9}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $9$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493850 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493970 1069 "q-324457" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493970 1071 "In a distribution center, the average weight of shipped packages is $2.5$ kilograms. However, $20%$ of packages weigh no more than $2.3$ kilograms. Assuming that package weights are normally distributed, find the standard deviation, $\\sigma ,$ of the package weights, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$kg$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493970 1072 #uuid "e22ccace-d75b-5bc7-acc2-7090937bcda1" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493970 1074 "Let $X$ represent the package weight (in kilograms). Then we have $X∼N(2.5,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 2.3)=0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.3) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.3-2.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ -\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)=-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & =0.8416 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.2}{0.8416} \\\\  & ≈0.24\\,\\text{kg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493875 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493971 1069 "q-132434" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493971 1071 "The volume of soda in cans filled at a particular factory is normally distributed with a mean of $12$ ounces and a standard deviation of $0.1$ ounces. What is the probability that a randomly selected soda can contains more than $12.2$ ounces? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9861\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ |" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493971 1072 #uuid "e312f711-a596-58e6-bba4-4f3bc71c5f9f" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493971 1074 "Let $X$ represent the volume (in ounces) of soda in a randomly selected can. Then we have $X∼N(12,{0.1}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X>12.2).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>12.2) & =P(Z>\\frac{12.2-12}{0.1}) \\\\  & =P(Z>2) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(2)=1-0.9772≈0.02.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected soda can contains more than $12.2$ ounces is approximately $0.02.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493884 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493972 1069 "q-162830" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493972 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=4x+2y-z$ on the intersection of the plane $x-y-z=2$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493972 1072 #uuid "e3b568f7-cd27-5a4c-97e4-f5b7033e39b7" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493972 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+2y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=2,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,2,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 2=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ -1=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $x=y,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(1,1)\\,\\text{or}\\,(x,y)=(-1,-1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =2 \\\\ 1-1-z & =2 \\\\ z & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =2 \\\\ -1-(-1)-z & =2 \\\\ -1+1-z & =2 \\\\ z & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,1,-2),\\,(-1,-1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,1,-2)$ | $(-1,-1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $8$ | $-4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,1,-2),$ and its maximum value is $8.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493865 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493973 1069 "q-82985" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493973 1071 "Using the change of variables $u=x+3y$ and $v=x+2y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le 2,\\,2\\le x+2y\\le 4\\}.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493973 1072 #uuid "e9c37ee3-304c-5283-97a5-75391f3d1e94" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493973 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=x+2y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |-1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493867 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493974 1069 "q-117710" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493974 1071 "![](images/d3/d3c5420ce272b26c0c80436f8e3d9a27b55e5ad41566b67c1c885e0048251a3f.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493974 1072 #uuid "ecf10c03-5a60-5be3-b6b8-bd61dcb4affd" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493974 1074 "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-3],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-3,-1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segments for $x\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,-1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-4.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =6-4+4-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493870 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493975 1069 "q-325123" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493975 1071 "The average amount of coffee dispensed into medium cups is $10$ ounces. Additionally, in $15%$ of cups, the coffee amount is less than $9.1$ ounces. Assuming the amount dispensed is normally distributed, find the percentage of cups containing less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.05$ | $0.10$ | $0.15$ | $0.20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ | $\\,0.0336\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ | $\\,0.0418\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ |\n\nApprximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493975 1072 #uuid "fe644563-13d6-5255-adb7-59462252c8b9" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493975 1074 "Let $X$ represent the amount of coffee in a randomly selected cup. Then we have $X∼N(10,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<8.5).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<9.1)=0.15.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9.1) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{9.1-10}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ \\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{0.9}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.9}{1.0364} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈0.8684.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<8.5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<8.5) & =P(X\\le 8.5) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{8.5-10}{0.8684}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.73) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.73) \\\\  & =0.0418 \\\\  & ≈4.18%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $4.18%$ of coffee cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee." 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493976 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494043 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493976 1062 #uuid "01cd63b9-1bc2-5c63-877d-2c84f5a29f2f" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493976 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064736 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493977 1058 #edb/ref 17592186493994 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493977 1062 #uuid "05338efe-276c-51da-8655-6d896721903f" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493977 1063 #edb/ref 17592186493954 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493978 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494066 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493978 1062 #uuid "0a7c3978-56f8-5be3-997f-dd9a4f1bdf46" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493978 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134288 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493979 1062 #uuid "1157fa95-5ca0-5552-8548-4e37f9ab0aa7" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493979 1063 #edb/ref 17592186370100 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493980 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494024 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493980 1062 #uuid "13d18f39-8c3e-50cf-926a-da6be8b2ee39" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493980 1063 #edb/ref 17592186493958 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493981 1062 #uuid "156e434a-b38e-5003-b3b2-3990ebd3f421" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493981 1063 #edb/ref 17592186351251 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493982 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494054 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186493982 1062 #uuid "1c778fa9-95bd-5205-85c3-55aecdf8bb5a" 1